---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import site, { waLink } from "../data/site.js";
import {
  professionalService,
  personDocument,
  breadcrumb,
} from "../lib/schema.js";

const jsonLd = [
  professionalService(),
  personDocument(),
  breadcrumb([
    { name: "Home", path: "/" },
    { name: "About", path: "/about/" },
  ]),
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`About ${site.personName}`}
  description={`${site.personName} is a one-person studio running three services from ${site.location.country} for clients in the US, Canada and Europe: photo editing and colour correction, custom web development, and polo shirt print pattern design.`}
  path="/about/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">About</span>
        <h1 class="h1"><span class="split">One person. Three things, done properly.</span></h1>
      </div>

      <figure class="page-figure rise">
        <img
          src={site.aboutImage.src}
          alt={site.aboutImage.alt}
          width={site.aboutImage.width}
          height={site.aboutImage.height}
          loading="eager"
          fetchpriority="high"
          decoding="async"
        />
        <figcaption>{site.personName} — every file is judged by eye on this screen.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          I am {site.personName}. I run a one-person studio from
          {" "}{site.location.country}, for clients in
          {" "}{site.areasServed.join(", ")}. It is not a studio floor and not a
          queue of freelancers — the person you message is the person who does
          the work, and everything is delivered online.
        </p>
        <p>
          Three services, each with its own page, its own pricing and its own
          turnaround:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong><a href="/pricing/#photo-editing">Photo editing and colour correction</a></strong>
            {" "}— retouching and grading for photographers, done by hand and
            priced by the photo. For weddings specifically, see
            {" "}<a href="/wedding-photo-editing/">wedding photo editing</a>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong><a href="/custom-web-development/">Custom web development</a></strong>
            {" "}— sites and web apps coded from scratch, no WordPress and no
            page builders. Fixed-price projects, and the code is handed to you.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong><a href="/apparel-pattern-tech-packs/">Apparel patterns and tech packs</a></strong>
            {" "}— production-ready patterns, grading and factory tech packs for
            small clothing brands.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Why by hand</h2>
        <p>
          The through-line across all three is that a person does the judging. In
          photo editing that means a colour grading suite painted neutral grey so
          the eye can read colour without being pulled one way or another. The
          screen is calibrated, the room is neutral, and every image is compared
          against a known reference before it is called done.
        </p>
        <p>
          AI editing tools are fast, but they apply a look. They guess at white
          balance, smooth skin into plastic, and drift across a set so the
          hundredth photo does not match the first. A person catches that. A
          person knows that the white dress is white, that the client&rsquo;s skin
          has a particular warmth, and that the light changed when the sun went
          behind a cloud halfway through the ceremony.
        </p>
        <p>
          It is the same instinct in the other two services: a website that is
          written rather than assembled from plugins, and a tech pack with
          nothing left for the factory to guess at.
        </p>

        <h2>The photo editing setup</h2>
        <ul>
          {site.tools.map((t) => <li><strong>{t.label}:</strong> {t.value}</li>)}
        </ul>

        <h2>What the photo editing covers</h2>
        <ul>
          {site.services.map((s) => <li>{s}</li>)}
          <li>Removing distractions — sensor dust, stray objects, blemishes</li>
          <li>Matching every frame in a set to one consistent look</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Who I work with</h2>
        <p>
          Photographers and studios, small business owners, and indie clothing
          brands in {site.areasServed.join(", ")}. Everything is handled online,
          so where you are does not matter. The Fiverr profile has a
          {" "}{site.fiverrStats.rating} rating over {site.fiverrStats.reviews}
          {" "}reviews and {site.fiverrStats.ordersPlus}+ completed orders.
        </p>

        <h2>Try it first</h2>
        <p>
          Photo editing comes with a free sample edit — {site.freeSample.line}
          {" "}Wedding galleries get the first 25 images free. Web and apparel
          projects start with a no-charge scope call before any price is agreed.
        </p>

        <h2>How you pay</h2>
        <p>
          Photo work is paid after delivery, never before — you see the edited
          photos, you approve them, then you are invoiced, by PayPal, Payoneer,
          Remitly, TapSend or bank deposit. Web and apparel projects are staged,
          with the split set out on each service page.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="btn-row" style="margin-top:2.5rem">
        <a class="btn" data-magnetic href={waLink("Hi Harinda, I read your About page and I'd like to talk about a project.")}>Message on WhatsApp</a>
        <a class="btn btn--grad" href="/pricing/">See pricing</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
